      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm currently conducting a stability study on a high-shear, low-fat mayonnaise emulsion optimized for high-acid environments (pH ~3.2). We are observing a non-linear increase in droplet coalescence rates that doesn't seem to align perfectly with standard DLVO predictions for the lecithin-based system we are using.\n\nCould you provide a rigorous analysis of the interfacial tension dynamics during this pH transition? Specifically, I’d like to explore the relationship between the surface potential ($\\psi_0$) and the Gouy-Chapman model in this context, particularly how the specific adsorption of protons and the resulting changes in the electrical double layer might be influencing the interfacial curvature and the effective steric repulsion of the lecithin-coated droplets. I'm trying to determine if we are seeing a breakdown in the steric layer due to a localized compression of the Debye length, or if there is a more complex interaction occurring at the molecular level of the headgroup-solvent interface.",
